<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Federalist Papers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1787">1787</a>–<a href="/years/1788">1788</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as The Federalist</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Federalist Papers are a collection of 85 articles and essays written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> and John Jay under the collective pseudonym Publius to promote the ratification of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a>. The first 77 appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> newspapers between October 1787 and April 1788, and the whole was published in two volumes by J. &amp; A. McLean in 1788. Among them, Madison's No. 10 argues against rule by a majority faction and for a large republic, his No. 51 for checks and balances, and Hamilton's No. 78 lays the groundwork for judicial review by federal courts. The Constitution was ratified by the required nine states on 21 June 1788.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-federalist-papers.jpg" alt="The Federalist Papers" width="330" height="535" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Publius (pseudonym) [ Alexander Hamilton, John Jay, James Madison ]. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Federalist_(1st_ed,_1788,_vol_I,_title_page)_-_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Federal Convention sent the proposed Constitution to the Confederation Congress, which submitted it to the states at the end of September 1787. Critics soon appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> press: Cato on 27 September and Brutus on 18 October, writings later grouped as the Anti-Federalist Papers. Alexander Hamilton decided to launch a defence and explanation of the Constitution for the people of the state of New York. He recruited John Jay, who wrote Nos. 2 to 5 and No. 64, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a>, who became his main collaborator. The series appeared under the name Publius, after Publius Valerius, a founder of the ancient republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p>The authors tried to hide their identities because Hamilton and Madison had attended the convention. The essays were written in haste, sometimes three or four in a week. Publisher J. &amp; A. McLean released the first 36 essays as a bound volume on 22 March 1788, and a second volume on 28 May contained Nos. 37 to 85.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>In No. 1 Hamilton listed six topics: the usefulness of the Union, the weakness of the Confederation, the need for an energetic government, the conformity of the Constitution to republican principles, its likeness to the state constitutions, and the security it would give to liberty. Madison's No. 10 discusses preventing rule by a majority faction and advocates a large commercial republic, and No. 51 gives the argument for checks and balances. Hamilton's No. 70 argues for a single chief executive and No. 78 lays the groundwork for judicial review of federal acts by federal courts. In No. 84 he opposes adding a bill of rights, fearing that a written list would be read as the only rights people had.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Constitution was ratified on 21 June 1788 by the nine states required. Historians doubt how much the essays contributed: they were not reliably reprinted outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, ten states had ratified before New York did on 26 July, and John Kaminski suggests that their effect on citizens of New York was negligible. Authorship was long uncertain. Hamilton's list, made before his duel with Aaron Burr, credited him with 63 essays, while Madison's list of 1818 claimed 29. Modern scholars generally credit Hamilton with 51, Madison with 29 and Jay with 5, with 12 disputed. Federal judges have used the essays as a contemporary account of the framers' intentions since Calder v. Bull in 1798.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Federalist Papers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
